{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888588,"nonce":"OYzkICFoosugFPzlblI16JGhDrg=","title":"The Investigator's org-wide controls are declared in ADR 0053 §6 but nothing enforces a daily budget, concurrency or a minimum interval","message":"**What is wrong.** §6 requires an org daily token budget (new runs recorded `skipped` with reason `budget`, never queued), max concurrent Investigations (wait, never dropped), and a per-Investigator minimum interval per subject (triggers inside it coalesce). `180a525` enforces only per-run budgets.\n\n**The demonstration.** A storm triggering repeated runs has no ceiling on spend.\n\n**Why it matters.** Cost and rate are the controls an operator reaches for first; \"recorded, never silent\" is the ADR's rule.\n\n**Where.** Org settings for the daily budget and concurrency; the Investigator's minimum interval; the job scheduler.\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** Past the daily budget, new Investigations exist as `skipped`/`budget` rows visible in the UI and reset at UTC midnight; concurrency excess waits; triggers within the interval produce one run; tests cover each.\n\n**Blocked by.** `180a525`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}